In his <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-07-26/apple-glasses-may-debut-at-wwdc-2027-privacy-camera-features-versus-meta-ms1v7lta">newsletter today</a>, <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman reiterated that a new "Apple Upgrade" program is set to launch in the U.S. on Tuesday, July 28. Apple will stop accepting <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/iphone/iphone-upgrade-program">iPhone Upgrade Program</a> sign-ups on the same day, but he said customers who are already enrolled will be able to remain in the program "for the time being."<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/07/iPhone-18-Pro-and-Pro-Max-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1055254" /><br/>
Apple is partnering with buy-now, pay-later company Klarna on the program, he said.<br/>
<br/>
The program will allow you to lease most iPhone, iPad, Mac, and Apple Watch models, with a 24-month term for iPhones and Apple Watches and a 36-month term for iPads and Macs. Customers will have various options, including returning their device after the term, upgrading to a newer device, paying a fee to keep the device, and so forth.<br/>
<br/>
Some lower-priced devices, including the Apple Watch SE, the entry-level iPad, the iPhone 16, and the MacBook Neo, will not be eligible, he said.<br/>
<br/>
Unlike the iPhone Upgrade Program, Apple Upgrade plans will apparently not include AppleCare+ coverage bundled into the monthly price.<br/>
<br/>
